      "outputTokens": 806,
      "costUsd": 0.0073095,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a home loan of four hundred thousand dollars at five per cent a year, compounded monthly. Repaying two thousand one hundred and forty-seven dollars twenty-nine a month clears it in thirty years. That time is called the term. A mortgage calculator shows it straight away. Now suppose you pay two thousand five hundred dollars a month instead. That's about three hundred and fifty dollars more. How much sooner is the loan gone? Less than a year, two to five years, or more than five years? Make a guess."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f10/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1158,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 1.0440000000000431,
        "after": "debt.",
        "before": "At"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "debt",
          "start": 15.93499999999999,
          "end": 16.128599999999988
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 38.6,
      "peakFraction": 0.8515625,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [
        {
          "word": "and",
          "start": 20.294000000000032,
          "end": 20.35400000000003,
          "score": 0.053
        },
        {
          "word": "eighty",
          "start": 20.375000000000032,
          "end": 20.57500000000003,
          "score": 0.288
        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 965,
      "costUsd": 0.0087565,
      "pronunciationText": "Look at the first month. The interest is charged on the four hundred thousand you owe, so it's one thousand six hundred and sixty-six dollars sixty-seven either way, rounded to the cent. Paying more can't change this month's interest. So every extra dollar goes straight to the debt. At the thirty-year repayment, the debt falls by four hundred and eighty dollars sixty-two. At two thousand five hundred, it falls by eight hundred and thirty-three dollars thirty-three, nearly twice as much. And it doesn't stop there. The lower balance means less interest next month, and every month after, so even more of each repayment goes to the debt."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f10/scene-03.wav",
