      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 137,
      "outputTokens": 958,
      "costUsd": 0.0086905,
      "pronunciationText": "To see the whole loan, let a digital tool do the months. A mortgage calculator, or a spreadsheet, runs the recurrence row after row: multiply by one plus r, then subtract the repayment. Slide the repayment up to two thousand five hundred, and the balance reaches zero at the two hundred and sixty-fifth repayment. That last one only needs five hundred and forty dollars fifty-seven. That's twenty-two years and one month, so the loan is gone about eight years sooner. So the answer to the guess is more than five years. That last repayment is smaller because, by then, what's owed plus its interest is less than a full repayment."
    },
    {
