      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 141,
      "outputTokens": 1026,
      "costUsd": 0.009304499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Why do the savings shrink? Only the part of a repayment above the interest pays the debt down. The first month's interest here is about one thousand six hundred and sixty-six dollars sixty-seven. Pay exactly that interest, and the balance stays at four hundred thousand for ever. That's called an interest-only repayment. Pay any less, and the balance grows. Now plot the term against the repayment. Just above the interest-only line, a small extra amount makes a huge difference, from about seventy-nine years at seventeen hundred dollars to about thirty-six at two thousand. Far above it, the curve flattens, and extra money buys only ay little time."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-06",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f10/scene-06.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1215,
