        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 137,
      "outputTokens": 1012,
      "costUsd": 0.0091765,
      "pronunciationText": "Now keep the repayment at two thousand five hundred and change the interest rate. Each rate is a separate loan, fixed from the first month. At five per cent, it takes about twenty-two years. At four per cent, about nineteen. At six per cent, about twenty-seven, and at seven per cent, nearly thirty-nine. So a rate rise of one point adds about five years, but a fall of one point saves only about three. Here's why. A higher rate raises the interest-only line, so less of the same repayment is left for the debt. And the closer that line gets to your repayment, the faster the term grows."
    },
